1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,273
An AI team does not ultimately want another
invoice for compute. It wants a model that

2
00:00:07,273 --> 00:00:13,182
performs better on a task it cares about.
GradientMine explores a different contract:

3
00:00:13,182 --> 00:00:18,636
define the improvement, commit the reward,
and inspect the evidence before settlement.

4
00:00:18,636 --> 00:00:22,727
We are building model-improvement bounties,
not a new cryptocurrency

5
00:00:22,727 --> 00:00:25,000
or a general-purpose GPU cloud.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
A task owner selects a frozen parent model
and commits the evaluation rules, deadline

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,714
and reward. Workers independently train
candidate updates. A named evaluator tests

8
00:00:35,714 --> 00:00:40,000
those candidates against the same held-out
examples and records signed,

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,286
content-addressed receipts. The Solana
escrow program binds the bounty terms,

10
00:00:44,286 --> 00:00:49,429
records candidate commitments and restricts
settlement to a registered winner. A creator

11
00:00:49,429 --> 00:00:55,000
has an on-chain timeout refund rather than a
promise from an unavailable server.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,735
The included task is intentionally small
enough to reproduce: a real neural digit

13
00:01:00,735 --> 00:01:06,912
classifier with a low-rank update trained on
a CPU. We run separate worker processes,

14
00:01:06,912 --> 00:01:11,324
compare actual before-and-after results,
inspect artifact hashes and verify the

15
00:01:11,324 --> 00:01:15,735
expected evaluator's signature. The
interface separates an eligible result from

16
00:01:15,735 --> 00:01:21,912
a paid result. Local runs never show pretend
token transfers. A Devnet payment needs

17
00:01:21,912 --> 00:01:25,000
finalized transaction evidence, not a green
animation.

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,667
The difficult part is trust. We do not claim
that a signature proves training, or that

19
00:01:31,667 --> 00:01:35,833
one server is decentralized verification.
The public digits benchmark is

20
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:39,167
reconstructible and unsuitable for
real-money competition. Our initial

21
00:01:39,167 --> 00:01:43,333
contribution is a coherent, inspectable
workflow: explicit terms, exact wallet

22
00:01:43,333 --> 00:01:47,083
transaction checks, independent experiments,
evidence downloads and documented recovery.

23
00:01:47,083 --> 00:01:51,667
We are building on established low-rank
adaptation and learning from verification

24
00:01:51,667 --> 00:01:55,000
research rather than claiming to have
invented them.

25
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Our first customer hypothesis is small
model-owning teams with a narrow, measurable

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:08,000
task and properly licensed data. We plan to
test the workflow with consenting design

27
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,000
partners and compare total cost, including
losing experiments, against ordinary

28
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,500
contracted fine-tuning. A future fee on
successful bounties is a business-model

29
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:23,500
hypothesis. We have internal engineering
evidence, not validated customer traction

30
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:27,500
yet. GradientMine: measure the work, make
the trust

31
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:30,000
visible, and reward the improvement.
